 Was it recently? I went out harvesting this year on the Sonoma coast in the first part of May. I think

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:14.440
 it was in like May 5th through 8th. It was early. It was still early for a harvest year, and that

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:18.040
 showed in the thickness of some of the varieties. Like the kombu was still a little thin.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:25.730
 The nori was just about an inch tall when it eventually will grow to be about a dinner plate

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:33.290
 size. So it felt early. But the bladder rack, which is another variety we collect, that one was looking

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:40.410
 perfect. It was before the full air bladders had formed on the little pads and before it started

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.050
 spoiling. What's spoiling?

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:49.850
 Spoiling. Spores. It's in the algae family, the seaweed, and so their way of reproduction is through spores.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:56.650
 And so they, "spoilates," I guess is the vocabulary word, little pustules on the leaf of just that

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:59.850
 way, a way to admit these spores into the water system.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:03.420
 Cool. I want to see one. I'll have to come sometime.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:10.320
 So I'm here today with Heidi Herman of Strongarm Farm, and we're going to start by talking about

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.840
 seaweed. I've always wanted to go seaweed collecting with you. I have bad timing that I can never actually

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.160
 make it when you're going to go, but one of these times they will. But I'd love to hear,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:26.900
 how'd you start? And maybe tell us about the first time you went looking for seaweed and what that was

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:27.060
 like.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:35.760
 Heidi Herman: I go primarily on the Sonoma Coast up here. My entire time with seaweed is up here.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:42.220
 I started about 10 years ago. I went with some friends that graduated from the California School

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:46.620
 of Herbal Studies in Forestville here in Sonoma County, and they took me out. I'm like, "Oh man,

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:51.900
 this is exciting." And I was just thrilled. And so it became this kind of annual thing with that set of

00:04:51.900 --> 00:05:00.080
 friends for the first few years. And then I started my farm, Strongarm Farm, as a produce and vegetable

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:08.110
 farm with cut flowers in about 2009. And that first season, I was at the Oxfam Farmers Market as a

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:14.190
 vendor, and I didn't have much on my table. I was a new farmer, and spring is a little light for a lot

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:18.670
 of farmers. Things were in the ground, but they hadn't come. I'm like, "What else could I sell and

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:25.710
 have on my table?" And so I had a lot of seaweed still at home. And so I packaged it up in a little

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:32.510
 Ziploc bag, and it was really well received. And there was no other people vending that product. And

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:38.190
 so it was total encouragement, just the public demand and excitement and the questions about it. And

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:44.350
 then I created a label. And the next week, I got better packaging. And then I got to go out more

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:50.940
 days than just this one. And so then it turned into three. And now we're up to about 11, 12 days a

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:56.890
 year I go out with a crew. And I hire people, and it's become a business.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:01.860
 Tell me about the days you go out. How do you select those days? And where do you go? What's it like?

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:09.460
 Yes. So yeah, I'm able to pick the days I will be harvesting early in the year. When I get the

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:14.180
 little tide book, you can get them at the Ace Hardware stores or at surf shops. And I look for

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:20.640
 the negative low tides in these little books. And those are the days to go. The negative low tide

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:26.640
 means the water is really receded out to about a couple hundred yards, more than it would be a

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.880
 normal, you know, a normal tide fluctuation. So these are real kind of extreme dates. And there's

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:39.070
 just a handful of them each year, maybe like 20 dates in total. But getting that to line up with

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:49.230
 daylight hours and summer, two qualifiers. So it kind of narrows down to, yeah, the 12 days I go out

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:54.190
 this year. And so I'll select those dates early in the year. When I'll be working, I set them aside,

00:06:54.190 --> 00:07:01.900
 then I start kind of recruiting for helpers of what days I'll be going out and the processing days.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:07.690
 And they're always around the June solstice is like the peak historical lowest tide of the year.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:18.910
 So mid June and May and July are also pretty great low tides. But similar to land plants, these algae that

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:26.270
 are in the ocean respond to day length, and so daylight hours, kind of the quality and quantity

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:30.830
 of hours, and so that in June, they're at their peak. And so similar to land plants, a lot of them

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:39.470
 kind of are in their peak before they get, before they go into reproduction stage, or they get battered

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:45.070
 by storms in the fall and winter. So there are also great low tides in December, but they're in the dark,

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:51.370
 and the algae is tattered and kind of worn out by then, not looking optimal.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:58.050
 So I'm a surfer, a really bad surfer. And so I spend a lot of time looking at seaweed not from waves.

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:05.170
 And I noticed that time of year, I mean, this time of year, right, in the beginning of summer,

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:11.960
 that the seaweed kind of feels different. It has kind of this lush quality to it that it doesn't

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:19.550
 at other times of year, like you're talking about. So what is like the, what is the life cycle of this,

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:26.450
 of the seaweed that you harvest? Like, how tall do they grow? What do they look like? What's the

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:26.850
 texture?

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:30.670
 Right. I wondered those same things. How are they all different?

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:37.720
 So there's each variety has its kind of a lot of different characteristics that are unique to it,

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:41.080
 similar to land plants, like say, where a fern would grow is different than where

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:46.660
 cactus would grow. And so, well, that's of course in relation to water. But

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:55.210
 so the seaweed grows in its different intertidal zones. Some are in the very kind of the outermost,

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:59.210
 which would be in the deeper water, which would be also submerged most of the time,

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:04.570
 except in these extreme negative low tides. So out there is the kombu and the wakame.

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:11.180
 And even further out is the bull kelp, which just never kind of leaves or exits the water.

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:18.630
 And so back in on the rocks where I go is the kombu and the wakame. And that they're,

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:25.040
 they're perennial seaweeds. And so they, they start from a big kind of root mass, which is called a

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:36.790
 hold fast. And they're about five to 10 feet long, each individual kind of algae mass. And they emerge

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:45.440
 from this kind of unified base. And then further up near the shore is where the nori and bladder rack

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:50.560
 exist. And nori is interesting because it's only annual seaweed or not the only, but one of the ones

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:57.630
 that I collect that it's an annual, which is, it's just so being a horticulturist and a land plant grower,

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:02.880
 I just was delighted to find that there's annual and perennial sea vegetables too.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:09.440
 So that was real thrilling. And I could comprehend their life cycle really easily and relate.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:16.190
 So the, the nori, as I mentioned to being a, an annual, it grows definitely in response to the

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:24.160
 day length, grows a lot faster than some of the other varieties. And, and so that, so that one has

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:30.000
 little spores that will kind of affix to the rocks each year and then grow from that. But the other

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.920
 ones that are perennials, they'll, they'll kind of stay as this permanent mass, kind of like a large

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:39.200
 oregano plant. It just kind of stays there and gets, creates more fronds and stems out of it.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:47.180
 And so right now I'm looking at two of these bags that you harvested here, ones of nori and ones of

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:53.480
 kombu. And as I know from eating them, nori is like much more delicate, right? So that sounds kind

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:57.640
 of like what you're describing with an annual and a perennial. I think of those kinds of different

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:04.220
 plants, like a lettuce or an oregano plant. How do they attach to the rocks? Like, how do you cut them?

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:08.060
 How do you know where? Too many questions all at once.

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:13.310
 All right. Yes, you're right. The, I relate to kombu is similar to like a lettuce leaf. Yeah,

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:17.230
 it's, it's kind of the size of a large lettuce leaf and it, and it's, it's real thin. It actually,

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:23.230
 it's one cell thick and it's really slickery in the hand because it's, it kind of feels like a wet

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:27.230
 saran wrap in a sense. And it kind of, when it's fresh, it kind of has that same sensation in the

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:32.830
 mouth as well. But when it dries out, it turns into this kind of black tissue paper kind of substance.

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:39.570
 And the kombu is more, and it's okay. Back to the nori, it's, it's mild and it's highest in protein.

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:44.810
 So it has a kind of like a mild kind of, like you said, like lettuces have a mild flavor and the kombu is,

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:54.790
 the kombu is thicker. It's, it's kind of leathery like I've related to it, like jerky. And, and it does

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:59.670
 take some more chewing and it cooks longer. It takes a longer time to kind of disintegrate in a meal.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:05.590
 And it has a stronger flavor and it, it imbues a lot of algins or alginates. And that's that

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:13.730
 quality that's real slippery that turns a watery soup into something thicker. And that also is able

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:20.000
 to carry a lot of flavor and this umami flavor as well. It's, it's rich in naturally occurring,

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:21.200
 occurring glutamates.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:26.160
 Cool. I have so many questions about that, but I think I'm going to jump back to farming.

